/.env
# Would otherwise be copied into the image by `COPY . /app` — it holds the
# production database password and the token signing key.
/.env.prod
/.deploy/
# Deploy tooling: it builds the image, it does not belong inside it.
/deploy/
# The whole directory. The service reads none of it — the documents are served
# out of the database, not the filesystem — and it holds the filled-in résumé,
# its metadata and the tailoring notes: contact details, per-bullet figures, and
# a former employer's internal scale and vendor stack. `COPY . /app` would
# otherwise bake all of that into an image pushed to Artifact Registry, where
# anyone with registry read access could pull it back out.
#
# Excluding by directory rather than by filename on purpose: the previous
# per-file list silently stopped covering resume.json and resume.metadata.json
# the moment those were added.
/docs/
.venv/
data/
.idea/
.coverage
.pytest_cache/
htmlcov/
__pycache__/
.ruff_cache/
.git/
.github/
/tests/
# clients/ is not part of this repository — the browser client lives in
# resume-mcp-api-clients, and a deployment that wants one copies it into the
# build context before this Dockerfile's `COPY . /app`. clients/web/index.html
# is therefore wanted in the image when it is there at all: CLIENT_HTML_PATH
# points at /app/clients/web/index.html and GET /client serves it.
#
# These two are not wanted, and are listed for whoever clones the client
# repository to ./clients to work on it: they are whatever web/build.sh last
# left on that machine, gitignored and therefore invisible to review, and
# `COPY . /app` would bake them in. A stale dist/ is the worse of the two — it
# would be served to real users as though it were reviewed source.
/clients/web/dist/
/clients/web/node_modules/

coverage.xml
